and who isn't, well, that's up to the merit badge counselor,

100
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:05,880
isn't it? That's not up to us.

101
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:09,920
And if, and only if, there was ample evidence to find

102
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:13,080
that there was something untoward going on here,

103
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:16,040
I don't know whether I would stir this particular pot.

104
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:19,800
I would better advise to observe what's going on,

105
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:25,000
talk to the scout and see if that changes the way

106
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,960
that you perceive the situation.

107
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:33,120
Along the same lines of scouts, advancement and their parents,

108
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:37,440
I had an email that I'll summarize by saying that it deals

109
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:42,640
with a scout who is very ambitious himself and has very ambitious

110
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:45,600
parents, apparently, and they're probably driving pretty hard

111
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:47,960
to get him to Eagle Scout.

112
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:51,760
He's involved in a lot of activities outside of scouting

113
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:55,640
And there are a few concerns that this raises

114
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:57,600
with his scout master.

115
00:07:57,640 --> 00:08:00,400
That's pretty broad strokes about the situation

116
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:03,280
And, at face value, like most of you,

117
00:08:03,280 --> 00:08:05,800
this kind of situation would rub me the wrong way

118
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:08,160
and I can identify with that sense of frustration.

119
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:11,920
But laying all the frustrations and the personalities aside,

120
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:15,680
a demanding thing to do at times. what are the real issues?

121
00:08:16,040 --> 00:08:20,840
Well, we know that scouts advance by fulfilling a list of requirements.

122
00:08:21,040 --> 00:08:22,400
of those requirements,

123
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:25,080
Most of them are defined metrics.

124
00:08:25,240 --> 00:08:27,640
In other words, there's not a whole lot of ambiguity

125
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:29,560
about how you fulfill the requirement.

126
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:32,760
If it's 10 nights of camping, you go camping for 10 nights,

127
00:08:32,840 --> 00:08:34,000
Not that big a deal-

128
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:38,480
And a few advancement requirements don't have a set metric

129
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:41,200
And so you have to make a judgment call as to whether a

130
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:43,039
scout has fulfilled them or not.

131
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:46,800
Now, I most commonly hear about these judgment calls when we

132
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:52,840
talk about active participation and quote leadership, unquote-

133
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,120
and more about that in a moment- positions,

134
00:08:55,400 --> 00:08:58,320
And I hear about them pretty often because these are two

135
00:08:58,320 --> 00:09:01,440
things that are not real easy to understand about scouting.

136
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:06,440
Now, whenever somebody asked me about quote leadership, positions,

137
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:09,640
unquote, I am quick to point out two things.

138
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:15,360
The first is is the requirements refer to positions of

139
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:17,720
responsibility, not leadership?

140
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:20,760
The guide to advancement explains the importance of

141
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:24,760
differentiating between leadership and responsibility and

142
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:27,200
puts emphasis on responsibility.

143
00:09:27,440 --> 00:09:31,520
The second thing is: the only rank requirement that explicitly

144
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:35,560
requires the demonstration of leadership is found in the

145
00:09:35,560 --> 00:09:36,480
Eagle project.

146
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:40,160
I also want to note that the guide to advancement discusses

147
00:09:40,160 --> 00:09:45,320
factoring in activities outside of scouting in our evaluations

148
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:49,000
of responsibility for others and an active participation.

149
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:52,280
So before I go further, it would be important for me to

150
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:56,320
note- I will have a link to the guide to advancement in the

151
00:09:56,320 --> 00:09:57,280
podcast notes.

152
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:01,200
If you have not spent significant time with the guide to

153
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:05,280
advancement and you haven't at least scanned it and read

154
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:08,920
those sections that are relevant to your work in scouting,

155
00:10:09,280 --> 00:10:10,560
you really need to do that,

156
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:13,840
And then you need to keep it handy so you can go back and

157
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:14,640
refer to them.

158
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:18,080
It's not because of the complexity of the rules,

159
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:21,320
which are kind of complex in some situations, but the

160
00:10:21,320 --> 00:10:25,640
principles that are very clearly expressed in the guide that

161
00:10:25,640 --> 00:10:28,160
inform the way that you work with your scouts.

162
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:30,560
These are really important things.

163
00:10:30,880 --> 00:10:33,520
Get the guide to advancement, look through it and

164
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:38,880
especially study and read those areas that concern your

165
00:10:38,880 --> 00:10:40,160
role in scouting

166
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:44,240
And these issues around participation and

167
00:10:44,280 --> 00:10:47,720
responsibility are something that are useful to discuss

168
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:48,800
with your fellow scouts.

169
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:52,680
I think most of us act on assumptions about these

170
00:10:52,680 --> 00:10:55,840
things that don't jive with the very clear directions

171
00:10:55,840 --> 00:10:58,000
we're given in the guide to advancement and those

172
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,600
assumptions run up against some of the things that the

173
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:03,600
guide to advancement says and that will cause a little

174
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:07,920
friction. but it's important to make sure that we are

175
00:11:07,920 --> 00:11:11,000
clear on exactly how these two things work

176
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:14,800
And once we're clear on that, we understand a broad

177
00:11:14,840 --> 00:11:18,040
underlying principle that advancement is not the

178
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:21,200
scout versus the judgment of adult volunteers.

179
00:11:21,320 --> 00:11:24,400
It's the scout and adult volunteers being

180
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:26,840
supportive versus the requirements.

181
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:28,080
I want to say that again,

182
00:11:28,200 --> 00:11:32,400
Advancement is not about the scout satisfying the

183
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:35,080
judgment of adult volunteers.

184
00:11:35,320 --> 00:11:39,240
It's about the scout and supportive adult volunteers

185
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:43,240
finding a way for the scout to satisfy the requirements.

186
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:46,480
We are all on the same side.

187
00:11:46,720 --> 00:11:49,480
Now, you and I did not write the rank requirements.

188
00:11:49,600 --> 00:11:52,640
We don't personally embroider patches to present to

189
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:53,440
our scouts.

190
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:56,240
These are something whether we agree with them or

191
00:11:56,240 --> 00:11:59,040
not, whether we particularly like them or not. it's

192
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:00,400
all part of the deal.

193
00:12:00,520 --> 00:12:04,000
It's all part of your commitment and loyalty

194
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,560
to the program to understand how these things

195
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:09,520
work and it's really tiresome and self-defeating

196
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:12,720
to devise our own hoops for our scouts to jump

197
00:12:12,720 --> 00:12:15,840
through or to impose our own judgment on the

198
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:19,760
scouts when there are better ways to get the job done.

199
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:21,880
In my book there are two basic approaches to

200
00:12:21,880 --> 00:12:23,640
advancement judgment calls.

201
00:12:23,680 --> 00:12:27,680
So the first one is establishing a metric or a

202
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:30,440
number that all scouts have to meet.

203
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:33,040
So when we're talking about participation, you

204
00:12:33,040 --> 00:12:35,880
got to make this percentage of events and this

205
00:12:35,880 --> 00:12:37,520
percentage of meetings, things like that.

206
00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:38,960
That's one solution.

207
00:12:39,040 --> 00:12:41,400
The second basic approach is setting an

208
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:44,040
individual goal, in consultation with the

209
00:12:44,040 --> 00:12:46,800
scout and sometimes, when it's appropriate, with

210
00:12:46,800 --> 00:12:47,599
their parents.

211
00:12:47,719 --> 00:12:50,800
Now, having tried both approaches, I prefer

212
00:12:50,800 --> 00:12:55,520
setting an individual goal with and not for the

213
00:12:55,520 --> 00:12:55,880
scout.

214
00:12:56,520 --> 00:12:59,360
So let me emphasize that we're setting a goal

215
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:02,280
with the scout, not for the scout.

216
00:13:02,800 --> 00:13:05,680
If we just come up with a metric that feels

217
00:13:05,680 --> 00:13:08,079
right and we say that's going to apply to all

218
00:13:08,079 --> 00:13:10,959
scouts, that's setting a goal for them.

219
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:14,520
If we instead take a very little bit of time,

220
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:17,760
listen to what a scout has to say, inquire of his

221
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:20,480
parents or his family when that's appropriate,

222
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:23,560
will come up with an individualized goal that

223
00:13:23,560 --> 00:13:26,080
makes a whole lot more sense, that will fulfill

224
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:27,680
the requirement just fine

225
00:13:27,840 --> 00:13:29,760
And that takes about, you know, 10 minutes.

226
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:32,160
We sit down, we talk to the scout about the

227
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:34,960
goals that they would set for themselves to

228
00:13:34,960 --> 00:13:38,560
fulfill the active and responsibility aspects

229
00:13:38,560 --> 00:13:39,440
of the requirements.

230
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:42,440
And that begins with questions, not statements.

231
00:13:42,560 --> 00:13:44,600
Just to give you a shorthand approach. if I

232
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:46,360
was doing this with a scout, I would sit down

233
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:48,560
with them and I would ask them to read me

234
00:13:48,680 --> 00:13:51,760
the requirements in question, while a scout

235
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:54,920
participate actively in your troop and patrol

236
00:13:54,920 --> 00:13:58,520
for X number of months or occupy a position

237
00:13:58,520 --> 00:14:00,480
of responsibility for X number of months.

238
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:03,280
So we would be very clear on the exact

239
00:14:03,520 --> 00:14:05,400
wording of the two requirements.

240
00:14:05,600 --> 00:14:07,520
And then, before I offered any, my thoughts

241
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:09,520
on it, I would ask the scout to explain

242
00:14:09,520 --> 00:14:11,120
the requirements to me and how they

243
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:13,640
understood them and what kind of goal

244
00:14:13,720 --> 00:14:15,800
they were setting for themselves to

245
00:14:15,800 --> 00:14:16,920
fulfill the requirement.

246
00:14:17,079 --> 00:14:19,400
And I would listen carefully to what they

247
00:14:19,400 --> 00:14:21,240
have to say, because what they have to say

248
00:14:21,240 --> 00:14:23,280
is going to reveal any number of things.

249
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:24,760
It's going to reveal something about the

250
00:14:24,760 --> 00:14:26,240
relationship that they have with their

251
00:14:26,240 --> 00:14:28,160
parents, if that's applicable. it's

252
00:14:28,160 --> 00:14:30,040
